ry large.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Conclusion","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Key findings:\n\nMost apps are free (92.6%) and rated Everyone (80.9%), and the average rating is 4.19, with 78.1% of rated apps at 4.0 or above.\nGame, Communication and Tools get the most installs, and the top 5 categories make up 59.2% of all installs.\nInstalls and rating have almost no link (correlation 0.051), so popular apps are not always the best rated. Ratings rise slightly with installs, from 4.03 for 1K to 10K installs up to 4.38 for 10M to 100M.\nPrice has little effect on rating until apps cost over 50 dollars, where the average drops to 3.91. Free apps get about 37 times more reviews than paid apps.\nLarger apps tend to have more installs (correlation 0.334), so app size does not stop users from downloading good apps.\nUsers praise apps that are easy to use and complain most about bugs, crashes and bad updates.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Recommendations:\n\nFocus on free apps in high demand categories like Game, Communication and Tools, and earn through ads or in app purchases.\nMake apps simple and easy to use, since this is what users praise most in high rated apps.\nTest updates carefully and fix bugs quickly, because crashes and broken updates are the top complaints in low rated apps.\nKeep apps updated regularly, as 63.3% of apps were updated within the last 6 months.\nKeep paid apps at a fair price, since very expensive apps get lower ratings.","metadata":{}}]}